#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41589c99-7f3e-58b6-aca3-8b6b3d5aebb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333387} {:answer/id #uuid "032bce0a-381c-56ae-b43a-ad018afac066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333388} {:answer/id #uuid "59a66390-e21e-57bd-83ff-6626fb5678c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333389} {:answer/id #uuid "732deb1b-73f9-513a-9224-02c44822ab49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333390} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2615af-c873-5101-999f-cb8e25463abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb2615af-c873-5101-999f-cb8e25463abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333391} :answer-field/id #uuid "da09a662-bd62-5029-aed0-11a8572d60f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43072e08-2c50-58fc-b70f-e161da4c2358" :question/math-academy-id "q-97306" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,-ia⟩=-i⟨a,a⟩$ for any nonzero vector  $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.  By the antilinearity of $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$  in the second argument we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,-ia⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,a⟩=i⟨a,a⟩\\ne -i⟨a,a⟩\n$$\n\n \nfor any nonzero vector  $a\\in {C}^{n}.$\n\n- Statement II  is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
